---
scope: src/service/install
status: aligned
description: "Plans and executes the cold-start scaffold that turns a fresh host project into an AIDE-ready project, organized as a fixed set of categories the consumer applies."
intent: >
  The install layer turns a cold host project into an AIDE-ready
  project through a fixed set of categories — methodology pointer,
  doc set, pipeline pieces, MCP registrations, brain provisioning,
  IDE wiring. Each category returns a structured plan of would-create,
  would-overwrite, would-skip, and exists steps; the consumer decides
  how to act on the plan. The service stays silent on cold-start
  creation, pauses only on overwrites or on decisions the filesystem
  cannot resolve, and treats user-owned configuration as untouchable
  on every run after the first. MCP server changes are prescriptions
  the consumer applies, never direct writes by this layer.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Each category returns a structured plan whose steps are tagged would-create, would-overwrite, would-skip, or exists. The plan is data the consumer interprets — this layer does not decide when to interrupt the user.
    - A category whose plan contains only would-create steps applies silently. A category whose plan contains any would-overwrite step pauses for confirmation; categories pause independently so the user decides one at a time.
    - Decisions the filesystem cannot resolve — framework detection, brain root location, MCP merge into an existing config, IDE choice — always pause for user input, regardless of whether anything would be overwritten.
    - MCP server registrations are returned as prescriptions naming the entry the host's config must contain. The consumer merges and writes; this layer never edits the host's MCP config directly.
    - User-owned configuration is untouchable after the first scaffold. Subsequent runs never return the user-owned config directory's contents as would-overwrite, never patch them, and never migrate them.
    - The brain MCP entry's wire shape is derived from the user-owned brain.aide rather than supplied by this package. The package ships a default template the user can edit; the install never owns launcher bytes.
  undesired:
    - A category that conflates cold-create and overwrite into the same path, so the consumer cannot tell whether the user has anything to lose. The plan must distinguish would-create from would-overwrite explicitly, every step.
    - A would-overwrite step applied silently, or a would-create step that interrupts the user. Either inverts the silent-on-create / pause-on-overwrite contract that defines this layer's user experience.
    - The install layer reaching into the host's MCP config and editing it instead of returning a prescription. Direct writes here put unrelated server entries at risk on malformed configs and break the consumer's visibility into the merge.
    - A subsequent run that overwrites, patches, or migrates anything under the user-owned config directory. After first scaffold, that directory is the user's surface — code-level changes to its contents are forbidden.
    - The package supplying the brain's launcher command, args, or backend identity instead of deriving them from brain.aide. This re-introduces the connector-enum coupling the methodology has explicitly retired.
    - A new install category added by extending the consumers' wire shapes instead of by adding to the category set this layer owns. The category model is the contract; the consumers must remain thin.
---
